otal, which must be allocated across all columns.\n\nBut the solution's argument seems to assume that we need at least one vertical line for each column where h_c > k, not necessarily h_c - k vertical lines. Let's examine: Suppose column c has h_c points. Sunny lines can cover at most k of them (since each sunny line can cover at most one point in that column). So there remain at least h_c - k points. These points must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical (cover all points in that column) or horizontal/slope -1 (cover at most one point per column). So to cover h_c - k points, we could use one vertical line (covers all) or use up to h_c - k horizontal/slope -1 lines (each covering one). But we have limited number of non-sunny lines total: n - k. So we need to allocate them across columns.\n\nThus the condition for feasibility is that there exists a selection of non-sunny lines (vertical/horizontal/slope -1) such that each column's uncovered points are covered. This is a combinatorial covering problem.\n\nThe solution's argument for k≥4: \"Since a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having k≥4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.\" This is not rigorous. Let's examine for n=5, k=4. According to this argument, we have n-k = 1 non-sunny line, which must be vertical. They claim that three rightmost columns already force a vertical line for column n-2 (i.e., column 3). But we have only one vertical line, so they claim we need at least one vertical line for column 3, but also need vertical lines for other columns? Let's examine.\n\nFor n=5, columns sizes: h_5=1, h_4=2, h_3=3, h_2=4, h_1=5. k=4. Sunny lines can cover at most 4 points per column. So columns