(k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≥ floor((k+1)/2)\n2) floor(k/q)+1 ≥ floor((k+1)/2)\n\nWe need to find integer p,q satisfying these.\n\nCondition 2: floor(k/q)+1 ≥ floor((k+1)/2). For odd k = 2m+1, floor((k+1)/2) = m+1. So we need floor((2m+1)/q) + 1 ≥ m+1 => floor((2m+1)/q) ≥ m => (2m+1)/q ≥ m => q ≤ (2m+1)/m = 2 + 1/m. So q ≤ 2 + 1/m. Since q is integer ≥1, possibilities: q = 1 or q = 2 (for any m≥1). For m=1 (k=3), q ≤ 3, so q could be 1,2,3. For m≥2 (k≥5), q ≤ 2 + 1/m 2.5, so q ≤ 2. So for k≥5, q can only be 1 or 2. For k=3, q could be 1,2,3. So for k≥5, q ∈ {1,2}.\n\nNow condition 1: floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 ≥ floor((k+1)/2) = m+1. So floor((2m)/|p+q|) ≥ m => (2m)/|p+q| ≥ m => |p+q| ≤ 2. So |p+q| ∈ {1,2}. So we have possibilities:\n\n- |p+q| = 2\n- |p+q| = 1\n\nNow combine with q ∈ {1,2}.\n\nCase q = 1:\n\n- If |p+q| = 2 => p+1 = ±2 => p = 1 or p = -3. So p = 1 (p+q=2) or p = -3 (p+q=-2). So lines: p=1,q=1 => slope 1; p=-3,q=1 => slope -3. But p=-3,q=1 yields p+q = -2, which is allowed. However, we need to check if p=-3,q=1 yields a sunny line (p ≠ -q => -3 ≠ -1, okay). So slope -3 line is allowed. Does it achieve the bound? Let's test for k=5. For p=-3,q=1, we have |p+q| = |-3+1| = 2, okay. The sum constraint bound is floor((5-1)/2)+1 = floor(4/2)+1 = 2+1 = 3. The x constraint bound is floor(5/1)+1 = 5+1 = 6, which is larger. So the limiting bound is sum constraint, giving at most 3 points. So slope -3 line can achieve up to 3 points. Does there exist a line with slope -3 that contains exactly 3 points of T_5? Let's try to find such a line.\n\nEquation: -3x - y = c => y = -3x - c. For x=1 => y = -3 - c; x=2 => y = -6 - c; x=3 => y = -9 - c; x=4 => y = -12 - c; x=5 => y = -15 - c. To have y positive, c must be negative large. Let's set c = -4 => y = -3x + 4. Points: (1,1),(2,-2) invalid. So only (1,1). c = -5 => y = -3x + 5 => (1,2),(2,-1) invalid. So only (1,2). c = -6 => y = -3x + 6 => (1,3),(2,0) invalid. So only (1,3). c = -7 => y = -3x + 7 => (1,4),(2,1),(3,-2). So (1,4) and (2