ng points must be at most 2 per column (since each sunny line can cover at most one per column). Actually with 2 sunny lines, each column can have at most 2 points uncovered (one per line). So the uncovered points must be arranged such that each column has at most 2 points, and also each row has at most 2 points (since each sunny line can cover at most one per row). Also each diagonal (x+y constant) can have at most 2 points uncovered (since each sunny line can cover at most one per diagonal). So we need to find a configuration of uncovered points that satisfies these constraints.\n\nBut we also need to ensure that the uncovered points are exactly those that cannot be covered by non-sunny lines.\n\nWe need to see if it's possible to have k=2 for any n≥3. The solution claims it's impossible, but we need to verify.\n\nWe can try to prove impossibility for k=2 for all n≥3. Let's attempt a general argument.\n\nLet’s denote the set of points as S = {(a,b) ∈ Z^2: a,b ≥ 1, a+b ≤ n+1}. There are n(n+1)/2 points.\n\nWe have n lines, k sunny, n-k non-sunny.\n\nNon-sunny lines are vertical (x = c), horizontal (y = c), or diagonal (x+y = d). Let's denote V = set of vertical lines used, H = set of horizontal lines used, D = set of diagonal lines used. So |V|+|H|+|D| = n-k.\n\nWe need to cover all points.\n\nObservation: Each vertical line x=c covers all points with a=c. Similarly, each horizontal line y=c covers all points with b=c. Each diagonal line x+y = d covers all points with a+b = d.\n\nThus the set of points covered by non-sunny lines is the union of columns in V, rows in H, and diagonals in D.\n\nNow, consider the points not covered by non-sunny lines. These must be covered by sunny lines. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, each row at most once, each diagonal at most once. So each sunny line can cover at most one point from each column, row, diagonal.\n\nThus the uncovered points must be such that they can be covered by k sunny lines, i.e., there exists a set of k lines each intersecting each column at most once, etc., covering all uncovered points.\n\nWe can think of a bipartite matching problem: each sunny line can be thought of as a matching between columns and rows (since a line is determined by a point in each column and row). But more concretely, we can think of the uncovered points as a set of points such that the maximum size of a matching (i.e., the minimum number of lines needed to cover them with sunny lines) is ≤ k. This is reminiscent of the problem of covering points with lines of a given slope set.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a counting argument: each sunny line can cover at most one point in each column. So across all sunny lines, the total number of points that can be covered in column i is at most k. So for each column i, the number of points not covered by non-sunny lines must be ≤ k. Similarly for each row j, the number of uncovered points in row j must be ≤ k. Also for each diagonal d, the number of uncovered points on that diagonal must be ≤ k.\n\nThus we need to choose V, H, D such that for each column i, the number of points in column i not covered by V∪H∪D ≤ k. Since V covers entire column i if i ∈ V, then uncovered points in that column are zero. If i ∉ V, then we need to cover points in that column using H and D. Horizontal lines cover entire rows, diagonal lines cover points across columns.\n\nThus we need to find a selection of V, H, D of size n-k such that each column i not in V has at most k points left uncovered after covering by H and D.\n\nSimilarly for rows and diagonals.\n\nNow, for k=2, we need to ensure each column not covered by a vertical line has at most 2 points uncovered after using horizontal and diagonal lines.\n\nWe have n-k = n-2 non-sunny lines. So we can use at most n-2 lines among vertical, horizontal, diagonal.\n\nWe need to see if we can cover all columns except maybe some with at most 2 points left uncovered.\n\nBut we also need to cover all points eventua