ll a=1..n. So points (n-3,1),(n-2,1),(n-1,1),(n,1) are covered.\n\nWe have diagonal x+y = n+1 covering points (a,b) with a+b = n+1. So for columns:\n\n- Column n-3: points (n-3,b) with b ≤ 4 (since a+b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ n+1-(n-3) = 4). Among these, diagonal includes point (n-3,4) because (n-3)+4 = n+1. So (n-3,4) is covered.\n\n- Column n-2: points (n-2,b) with b ≤ 3. Diagonal includes (n-2,3) because (n-2)+3 = n+1. So (n-2,3) covered.\n\n- Column n-1: points (n-1,b) with b ≤ 2. Diagonal includes (n-1,2). So (n-1,2) covered.\n\n- Column n: point (n,1) covered by both horizontal and diagonal.\n\nNow we need to check remaining points in columns n-3, n-2, n-1, n after covering with horizontal y=1 and diagonal x+y=n+1.\n\nList all points in those columns:\n\nColumn n-3: points (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),(n-3,4). (n-3,1) covered by horizontal y=1. (n-3,4) covered by diagonal. Points (n-3,2),(n-3,3) remain uncovered.\n\nColumn n-2: points (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). (n-2,1) covered by horizontal. (n-2,3) covered by diagonal. (n-2,2) remains uncovered.\n\nColumn n-1: points (n-1,1),(n-1,2). (n-1,1) covered by horizontal. (n-1,2) covered by diagonal. So column n-1 fully covered.\n\nColumn n: point (n,1) covered.\n\nThus uncovered points after using vertical lines for columns 1..n-4, horizontal y=1, diagonal x+y=n+1 are: (n-3,2),(n-3,3),(n-2,2). That's 3 points. We have used n-4 vertical lines + 1 horizontal + 1 diagonal = n-2 non-sunny lines (since n-4+2 = n-2). So we have exactly n-2 non-sunny lines, leaving k=2 sunny lines to cover the remaining 3 points. As before, we have 3 points in columns n-3 and n-2: (n-3,2),(n-3,3) in column n-3, and (n-2,2) in column n-2. We need to cover these with 2 sunny lines. Is that possible? Let's see.\n\nWe have columns: n-3 has two points (n-3,2) and (n-3,3). Column n-2 has one point (n-2,2). So we need to cover these three points with 2 sunny lines. As before, we cannot cover both points in column n-3 with a single sunny line (vertical). So we need