sses through (3,? ) maybe (3,? ) = y = -2/3*(x-2)+3 = -2/3*(1)+3 = 3 - 2/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. So L3 covers (2,3) and (5,1). Good.\n\n- L4: passes through (2,4) and (3,1) maybe? Slope (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, sunny. This line also passes through (4,-2) not required. So L4 covers (2,4) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow check coverage: Column 2 points all covered: (2,1) L1, (2,2) L2, (2,3) L3, (2,4) L4. Column 3 points: (3,1) L4, (3,2) L1, (3,3) not covered. Column 4 points: (4,1) L2, (4,2) not covered. Column 5 point: (5,1) L3. So we still need to cover (3,3) and (4,2). We have no lines left. So we need to adjust assignments to also cover those.\n\nWe could try to adjust L1 to cover (3,3) instead of (3,2). Let's see line through (2,1) and (3,3) slope (3-1)/(3-2) = 2, sunny. That line also passes through (4,5) not required. So L1 could be (2,1)-(3,3). Then (3,2) remains uncovered. But maybe another line can cover (3,2). Let's see L2 currently covers (2,2)-(4,1). Could we change L2 to cover (3,2) as well? Let's see line through (2,2) and (3,2) is horizontal, not allowed. So cannot. L3 covers (2,3)-(5,1). Could we change L3 to cover (3,2) maybe? Let's see line through (2,3) and (3,2) slope (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. So cannot. L4 covers (2,4)-(3,1). Could we change L4 to cover (3,2) maybe? Line through (2,4) and (3,2) slope (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, sunny. That line also passes through (4,0) not required. So L4 could be (2,4)-(3,2). Then (3,1) remains uncovered. But maybe L2 could cover (3,1) instead of (4,1). Let's see line through (2,2) and (3,1) slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. So cannot. L1 could cover (3,1) maybe? L1 currently (2,1)-(3,3). Could we change L1 to (2,1)-(3,1) horizontal, not allowed. So not possible.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nWe need a systematic approach. Let's think about the constraints: With one non-sunny line (vertical x=1), we have 4 sunny lines to cover points in columns 2-5. The total number of points in columns 2-5 is 10. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most one point, so each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So total capacity of 4 lines is at most 4*4 = 16 points, which is enough. But we need to ensure that each column's points are covered. The main difficulty is that each line can only cover at most one point per column, but we need to cover multiple points per column. So we need to assign each column's points to distinct lines. For column 2 (4 points), we need each of the 4 lines to intersect column 2 at a distinct point. So each line must have a point in column 2. Similarly, column 3 has 3 points, so three of the four lines must intersect column 3 at distinct points. Column 4 has 2 points, so two lines must intersect column 4 at distinct points. Column 5 has 1 point, so one line must intersect column 5 at that point. So we need to assign each line a set of points across columns such that each line has exactly one point in column 2, and possibly also points in other columns, but cannot have two points in the same column.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines L1-L4 such that:\n\n- For each i=1..4, L_i passes through a distinct point in column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4) respectively.\n\n- Additionally, we need to assign points in column 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) to three of the lines (maybe each line can have at most one column 3 point). So we need to assign each of these three points to a line that already has a column 2 point, such that the line through those two points is sunny (i.e., not horizontal, vertical, slope -1). Similarly for column 4: (4,1),(4,2) to two lines (maybe same lines as column 3 or different). And column 5: (5,1) to one line.\n\nThus we need to find a matching of points across columns such that each line passes through a point in column 2 and possibly points in other columns, but the line must be straight (i.e., all points assigned to a line must be collinear). So we need to partition the set of points into at most 4 lines, each line containing exactly one point from column 2, and possibly other points from other columns, such that each line is not parallel to axes or slope -1.\n\nThis is a combinatorial geometry problem. Let's try to find such a partition.\n\nWe have column 2 points: A1=(2,1), A2=(2,2), A3=(2,3), A4=(2,4). Column 3 points: B1=(3,1), B2=(3,2), B3=(3,3). Column 4 points: C1=(4,1), C2=(4,2). Column 5 point: D1=(5,1).\n\nWe need to assign each line L_i a point from column 2 (Ai). Then we can optionally assign a point from column 3, 4, 5 to each line, ensuring collinearity.\n\nWe can think of constructing lines through each Ai and some other point(s). Let's consider each Ai and see which points in later columns can be collinear with it.\n\n- For A1=(2,1): line through A1 and B1=(3,1) is horizontal (slope 0) not allowed. A1 and B2=(3,2): slope (2-1)/(3-2)=1, sunny. A1 and B3=(3,3): slope (3-1)/(3-2)=2, sunny. A1 and C1=(4,1): slope (1-1)/(4-2)=0, not allowed. A1 and C2=(4,2): slope (2-1)/(4-2)=1/2=0.5, sunny. A1 and D1=(5,1): slope (1-1)/(5-2)=0, not allowed. So A1 can be paired with B2, B3, C2.\n\n- For A2=(2,2): line with B1=(3,1): slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. With B2=(3,2): horizontal, not allowed. With B3=(3,3): slope (3-2)/(3-2)=1, sunny. With C1=(4,1): slope (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. With C2=(4,2): slope (2-2)/(4-2)=0, not allowed. With D1=(5,1): slope (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333, sunny.\n\n- For A3=(2,3): line with B1=(3,1): slope (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, sunny. With B2=(3,2): slope (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. With B3=(3,3): horizontal, not allowed. With C1=(4,1): slope (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. With C2=(4,2): slope (2-3)/(4-2) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. With D1=(5,1): slope (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3 ≈ -0.666, sunny.\n\n- For A4=(2,4): line with B1=(3,1): slope (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, sunny. With B2=(3,2): slope (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, sunny. With B3=(3,3): slope (3-4)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. With C1=(4,1): slope (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. With C2=(4,2): slope (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. With D1=(5,1): slope (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus we have many possible pairings.\n\nNow we need to assign each column 3 point B1,B2,B3 to some line (maybe same line as column 2 point). Similarly for column 4 points C1,C2 and D1.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each must have a distinct column 2 point. Let's denote lines L1-L4 correspond to A1-A4 (maybe not in order). We need to assign B1,B2,B3 to three of the lines, C1,C2 to two lines, D1 to one line, ensuring collinearity.\n\nWe can try to assign:\n\n- L1: A1=(2,1) with B2=(3,2) (slope 1). Also maybe C2=(4,2)? Let's check if (2,1),(3,2),(4,2) are collinear. The line through (2,1) and (3,2) is y = x - 1? Actually slope 1, passes through (2,1) => y-1 = 1*(x-2) => y = x - 1. At x=4, y=3, not 2. So (4,2) not on that line. So cannot include C2. Could include D1? At x=5, y=4, not 1. So no. So L1 can only have A1 and B2 (maybe also B3? Let's check if (2,1),(3,2),(3,3) collinear? No, B3 not collinear with A1 and B2 because B2 and B3 have same x coordinate? Actually B2 is (3,2), B3 is (3,3). They are vertical line x=3, not collinear with A1. So L1 can only have A1 and B2.\n\n- L2: A2=(2,2) with B3=(3,3) (slope 1). At x=4, y=4, not a required point. So L2 covers A2 and B3.\n\n- L3: A3=(2,3) with B1=(3,1) (slope -2). At x=4, y = -2*(4-2)+3 = -4+3 = -1, not required. So L3 covers A3 and B1.\n\n- L4: A4=(2,4) with C1=(4,1) (slope (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2 = -1.5). At x=5, y = -1.5*(5-2)+4 = -1.5*3+4 = -4.5+4 = -0.5, not required. So L4 covers A4 and C1.\n\nNow we have covered all column 2 points (A1-A4), all column 3 points (B1-B3). We still need to cover column 4 point C2=(4,2) and column 5 point D1=(5,1). We have no lines left. Could we adjust assignments to include C2 and D1 in some lines? Let's see if any line can be extended to include C2 or D1 while preserving collinearity.\n\nCheck L1: line through A1=(2,1) and B2=(3,2) is y = x - 1. At x=4, y=3, not C2 (4,2). At x=5, y=4, not D1 (5,1). So cannot.\n\nL2: line through A2=(2,2) and B3=(3,3) is y = x? Actually slope 1, passes through (2,2) => y = x. At x=4, y=4, not C2 (4,2). At x=5, y=5, not D1.\n\nL3: line through A3=(2,3) and B1=(3,1) slope -2, equation y - 3 = -2(x-2) => y = -2x + 7. At x=4, y = -8+7 = -1, not C2. At x=5, y = -10+7 = -3, not D1.\n\nL4: line through A4=(2,4) and C1=(4,1) slope -1.5, equation y - 4 = -1.5(x-2) => y = -1.5x + 7. At x=5, y = -7.5+7 = -0.5, not D1.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nWe need to incorporate C2 and D1 into some lines, perhaps by pairing them with column 2 points directly, and then maybe pairing column 3 points with other column 2 points.\n\nLet's try a different approach: assign each line to a column 2 point and a column 4 point (or column 5 point) as needed, and then assign column 3 points to lines that already have column 2 points.\n\nWe have 4 lines, need to cover C2 and D1. So two of the lines must include C2 and D1 respectively. Let's try:\n\n- L1: A1=(2,1) with C2=(4,2). Check slope: (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 = 0.5, sunny. So L1 covers A1 and C2.\n\n- L2: A2=(2,2) with D1=(5,1). Slope (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333, sunny. So L2 covers A2 and D1.\n\nNow we need to cover B1,B2,B3 (column 3) and C1 (column 4) using the remaining two lines L3 and L4, each must have a distinct column 2 point (A3 and A4). So L3: A3=(2,3) with maybe B1=(3,1) slope -2, sunny. L4: A4=(2,4) with maybe B2=(3,2) slope (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, sunny. But we also need to cover B3=(3,3) and C1=(4,1). We have no lines left. Could we adjust L3 or L4 to also cover B3 or C1? Let's see if line through A3=(2,3) and B1=(3,1) also passes through C1=(4,1)? The line through (2,3) and (3,1) has slope -2, equation y - 3 = -2(x-2) => y = -2x + 7. At x=4, y = -8+7 = -1, not 1. So not.\n\nLine through A4=(2,4) and B2=(3,2) slope -2, equation y - 4 = -2(x-2) => y = -2x + 8. At x=4, y = -8+8 = 0, not 1. So not.\n\nAlternatively, we could pair A3 with B3 maybe? A3=(2,3) and B3=(3,3) horizontal, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nWe could pair A4 with B3? A4=(2,4) and B3=(3,3) slope -1, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nThus we need to incorporate B3 and C1 into lines L1 or L2 perhaps. Let's see if L1 (A1=(2,1) and C2=(4,2)) can also pass through B3=(3,3)? Let's compute line through (2,1) and (4,2): slope (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 = 0.5, equation y - 1 = 0.5(x-2) => y = 0.5x. At x=3, y = 1.5, not 3. So no.\n\nL1 with B3? line through (2,1) and (3,3) slope (3-1)/(3-2) = 2, equation y - 1 = 2(x-2) => y = 2x - 3. At x=4, y = 5, not 2. So not.\n\nL2 (A2=(2,2) and D1=(5,1)) slope -1/3, equation y - 2 = -1/3 (x-2) => y = -1/3 x + 8/3. At x=3, y = -1 + 8/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not 3. So not.\n\nL2 with B3? line through (2,2) and (3,3) slope 1, equation y - 2 = 1*(x-2) => y = x. At x=5, y=5, not 1. So not.\n\nL2 with C1? line through (2,2) and (4,1) slope (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2 = -0.5, equation y - 2 = -0.5(x-2) => y = -0.5x + 3. At x=5, y = -2.5+3 = 0.5, not 1. So not.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nWe need to find a different set of pairings.\n\nPerhaps we can use a non-sunny line that is not vertical, maybe diagonal x+y=5 or x+y=6, which covers many points across columns, reducing the load on sunny lines. Let's try using non-sunny line x+y=5 (slope -1). That covers (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Then we have 4 sunny lines to cover remaining points: column 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,5) (4 points). Column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,4) (3 points). Column 3: (3,1),(3,3) (2 points). Column 4: (4,2) (1 point). Column 5: (5,1) (1 point). Total 4+3+2+1+1 = 11 points. 4 lines can cover at most 4 points per column, but we need to cover column 1's 4 points, each line must intersect column 1 at a distinct point. So each line will have a point in column 1. Then we need to assign column 2 points to lines (3 points) - three of the four lines will have a column 2 point. Column 3 points (2 points) - two lines will have column 3 points. Column 4 point (1) - one line will have column 4 point. Column 5 point (1) - one line will have column 5 point. So we need to assign these points to lines such that each line is collinear with its assigned points.\n\nLet's denote column 1 points: A1=(1,1), A2=(1,2), A3=(1,3), A4=(1,5). Column 2 points: B1=(2,1), B2=(2,2), B3=(2,4). Column 3 points: C1=(3,1), C2=(3,3). Column 4 point: D1=(4,2). Column 5 point: E1=(5,1).\n\nWe need to assign each line Li to a distinct Ai, and optionally to a Bi, Ci, D1, E1.\n\nWe need to find lines through Ai and some other points.\n\nLet's compute possible pairings:\n\n- A1=(1,1) with B1=(2,1): horizontal, not allowed. With B2=(2,2): slope (2-1)/(2-1)=1, sunny. With B3=(2,4): slope (4-1)/(2-1)=3, sunny. With C1=(3,1): slope (1-1)/(3-1)=0, not allowed. With C2=(3,3): slope (3-1)/(3-1)=2, sunny. With D1=(4,2): slope (2-1)/(4-1)=1/3 ≈ 0.333, sunny. With E1=(5,1): slope (1-1)/(5-1)=0, not allowed.\n\n- A2=(1,2) with B1=(2,1): slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. With B2=(2,2): horizontal, not allowed. With B3=(2,4): slope (4-2)/(2-1)=2, sunny. With C1=(3,1): slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. With C2=(3,3): slope (3-2)/(3-1) = 1/2 = 0.5, sunny. With D1=(4,2): slope (2-2)/(4-1)=0, not allowed. With E1=(5,1): slope (1-2)/(5-1) = -1/4 = -0.25, sunny.\n\n- A3=(1,3) with B1=(2,1): slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. With B2=(2,2): slope (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. With B3=(2,4): slope (4-3)/(2-1) = 1, sunny. With C1=(3,1): slope (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. With C2=(3,3): horizontal, not allowed. With D1=(4,2): slope (2-3)/(4-1) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333, sunny. With E1=(5,1): slope (1-3)/(5-1) = -2/4 = -0.5, sunny.\n\n- A4=(1,5) with B1=(2,1): slope (1-5)/(2-1) = -4, sunny. With B2=(2,2): slope (2-5)/(2-1) = -3, sunny. With B3=(2,4): slope (4-5)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. With C1=(3,1): slope (1-5)/(3-1) = -4/2 = -2, sunny. With C2=(3,3): slope (3-5)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. With D1=(4,2): slope (2-5)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. With E1=(5,1): slope (1-5)/(5-1) = -4/4 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus many possible pairings.\n\nNow we need to assign each line to a distinct Ai and also assign B's, C's, D1, E1.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each must have a distinct Ai. Let's try to assign:\n\n- L1: A1=(1,1) with B2=(2,2) (slope 1). Also maybe C2=(3,3) is collinear? Let's check: line through (1,1) and (2,2) is y=x. At x=3, y=3, so C2=(3,3) lies on it. So L1 can cover A1, B2, C2. Good. Also D1? At x=4, y=4, not D1. E1? At x=5, y=5, not E1.\n\n- L2: A2=(1,2) with B3=(2,4) (slope 2). Equation: y-2 = 2(x-1) => y = 2x. At x=3, y=6 (not C1 or C2). At x=4, y=8 (not D1). At x=5, y=10 (not E1). So L2 covers A2 and B3.\n\n- L3: A3=(1,3) with B1=(2,1) (slope -2). Equation: y-3 = -2(x-1) => y = -2x +5. At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1 (not C1 or C2). At x=4, y = -8+5 = -3 (not D1). At x=5, y = -10+5 = -5 (not E1). So L3 covers A3 and B1.\n\n- L4: A4=(1,5) with C1=(3,1) (slope (1-5)/(3-1) = -4/2 = -2). Equation: y-5 = -2(x-1) => y = -2x +7. At x=2, y = -4+7 = 3 (point (2,3) not needed because (2,3) is covered by non-sunny line x+y=5). At x=4, y = -8+7 = -1 (not D1). At x=5, y = -10+7 = -3 (not E1). So L4 covers A4 and C1.\n\nNow we have covered all Ai (A1-A4), all Bi (B1-B3) except B2? Actually B2 is covered by L1. So all B's covered. C2 covered by L1, C1 covered by L4. D1 (4,2) and E1 (5,1) remain uncovered. We have no lines left. So we need to adjust to include D1 and E1.\n\nMaybe we can assign D1 to L2 or L3 or L4 by pairing with appropriate Ai.\n\nCheck possible pairings for D1=(4,2) with Ai:\n\n- With A1=(1,1): slope (2-1)/(4-1) = 1/3 ≈ 0.333, sunny. So line through A1 and D1 is possible. Does it also pass through any other required point? At x=2, y = 1 + (1/3)*(2-1) = 1 + 1/3 = 4/3 ≈ 1.333, not a required point. At x=3, y = 1 + (1/3)*(3-1) = 1 + 2/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not required. So L1 could be A1-D1 instead of A1-B2-C2. But we need to cover B2 and C2 as well. Maybe we can assign B2 and C2 to other lines.\n\n- With A2=(1,2): slope (2-2)/(4-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed.\n\n- With A3=(1,3): slope (2-3)/(4-1) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333, sunny. So line A3-D1 possible.\n\n- With A4=(1,5): slope (2-5)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed.\n\nNow E1=(5,1) with Ai:\n\n- With A1=(1,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n\n- With A2=(1,2): slope (1-2)/(5-1) = -1/4 = -0.25, sunny.\n\n- With A3=(1,3): slope (1-3)/(5-1) = -2/4 = -0.5, sunny.\n\n- With A4=(1,5): slope (1-5)/(5-1) = -4/4 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus we can pair D1 with A1 or A3, and E1 with A2 or A3.\n\nWe need to incorporate D1 and E1 into lines while still covering all other points.\n\nLet's try a new assignment:\n\nGoal: Use lines L1-L4 each with a distinct Ai. Let's try to assign:\n\n- L1: A1=(1,1) with D1=(4,2). Slope 1/3. This line also passes through maybe B? At x=2, y = 1 + (1/3)*(2-1) = 4/3, not integer. At x=3, y = 1 + (1/3)*(3-1) = 1 + 2/3 = 5/3, not integer. So L1 covers only A1 and D1.\n\n- L2: A2=(1,2) with E1=(5,1). Slope -1/4. At x=2, y = 2 - 1/4 = 1.75, not integer. At x=3, y = 2 - 2/4 = 1.5, not integer. At x=4, y = 2 - 3/4 = 1.25, not integer. So L2 covers only A2 and E1.\n\n- L3: A3=(1,3) with B1=(2,1) (slope -2). This line also passes through maybe C1? At x=3, y = -2*2 + 5 = 1, which is C1=(3,1). Indeed line through (1,3) and (2,1) passes through (3,-1)? Wait compute: line through (1,3) and (2,1) slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2. Equation: y - 3 = -2(x-1) => y = -2x +5. At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1, not (3,1). So I made mistake. Let's recompute: (1,3) to (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2. So line is y = -2x +5. At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1. So not C1. So L3 covers A3 and B1.\n\n- L4: A4=(1,5) with B2=(2,2) maybe? Slope (2-5)/(2-1) = -3, sunny. Equation: y - 5 = -3(x-1) => y = -3x +8. At x=3, y = -9+8 = -1, not required. At x=4, y = -12+8 = -4. So L4 covers A4 and B2.\n\nNow we have covered A1-A4, D1, E1, B1, B2. Still need to cover B3=(2,4), C1=(3,1), C2=(3,3), and maybe other points: B3, C1, C2. Also need to cover maybe (2,2) is covered by L4? Actually B2 is (2,2) covered. B3 is (2,4) not covered. C1 is (3,1) not covered. C2 is (3,3) not covered. Also we need to cover maybe (2,3) but that's covered by non-sunny line x+y=5? Actually (2,3) is covered by non-sunny line x+y=5 (since 2+3=5). So okay. Also (3,2) is covered by non-sunny line x+y=5. So we need to cover B3, C1, C2.\n\nWe have no lines left. So we need to adjust assignments to incorporate these points.\n\nMaybe we can pair B3 with A2? Let's see line through A2=(1,2) and B3=(2,4) slope 2, sunny. That line also passes through maybe C2? At x=3, y = 2*3 -? Actually line through (1,2) and (2,4) slope 2, equation y - 2 = 2(x-1) => y = 2x. At x=3, y=6, not C2. So not.\n\nAlternatively, pair C2 with A1? A1=(1,1) and C2=(3,3) slope (3-1)/(3-1)=1, sunny. That line also passes through B2? At x=2, y=2, which is B2=(2,2). So line through A1, B2, C2 is y=x. So we could use L1 = A1-B2-C2. That would cover A1, B2, C2. Then we need to cover D1 and E1 maybe with other lines.\n\nLet's try a new assignment:\n\n- L1: A1=(1,1), B2=(2,2), C2=(3,3). (y=x). Good.\n\n- L2: A2=(1,2), B3=(2,4). Slope 2, line y = 2x? Actually line through (1,2) and (2,4) is y = 2x? At x=1, y=2; at x=2, y=4. So yes, y=2x. At x=3, y=6 (not needed). So L2 covers A2, B3.\n\n- L3: A3=(1,3), B1=(2,1). Slope -2, line y = -2x +5. At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1 (not needed). So L3 covers A3, B1.\n\n- L4: A4=(1,5), D1=(4,2). Slope (2-5)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So cannot pair A4 with D1. Let's try A4 with C1=(3,1). Slope (1-5)/(3-1) = -4/2 = -2, sunny. So L4: A4=(1,5), C1=(3,1). Equation: y - 5 = -2(x-1) => y = -2x +7. At x=2, y = -4+7 = 3 (point (2,3) which is covered by non-sunny line). At x=4, y = -8+7 = -1 (not needed). So L4 covers A4 and C1.\n\nNow we have covered A1-A4, B1-B3, C2, C1. D1 and E1 remain uncovered. We have no lines left. So we need to incorporate D1 and E1 into some lines, perhaps by replacing some assignments.\n\nMaybe we can pair D1 with A2? But A2 already used in L2. Could we modify L2 to include D1? Let's see line through A2=(1,2) and D1=(4,2) is horizontal, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nPair D1 with A3? A3=(1,3) and D1=(4,2) slope (2-3)/(4-1) = -1/3, sunny. So line through A3 and D1: equation y - 3 = -1/3 (x-1) => y = -1/3 x + 10/3. At x=2, y = -2/3 + 10/3 = 8/3 ≈ 2.667, not integer. At x=3, y = -1 + 10/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333. So not covering any other required point. So we could replace L3 (A3-B1) with L3' = A3-D1. Then B1 remains uncovered. Could we cover B1 with another line? Maybe L4 could be changed to cover B1 and something else.\n\nLet's try a systematic approach: we need to cover 4 Ai, 3 Bi, 2 Ci, D1, E1 = total 11 points. 4 lines can cover at most 4 points each, but each line can cover multiple points if collinear. So we need to find a set of 4 lines that collectively cover all 11 points. This is a covering problem. Since we have many degrees of freedom, it's plausible that such a covering exists. But we need to find one.\n\nAlternatively, maybe using a different non-sunny line (like x+y=6) might simplify. Let's try non-sunny line x+y=6 (covers (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)). That covers many points: A4=(1,5), B3=(2,4), C2=(3,3), D1=(4,2), E1=(5,1). That's 5 points covered by the non-sunny line. Then we have 4 sunny lines left to cover remaining points: column 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) (4 points). Column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) (3 points). Column 3: (3,1),(3,2) (2 points). Column 4: (4,1) (1 point). Column 5: (5,? ) none left because (5,1) covered. So total uncovered points = 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. 4 lines can cover at most 4 points per column, but we need to cover column 1's 4 points, each line must intersect column 1 at a distinct point. So each line will have a point in column 1. Then we need to assign column 2 points (3) to three of the lines, column 3 points (2) to two lines, column 4 point (1) to one line. So we need to assign these points to lines such that each line is collinear with its assigned points.\n\nNow we have column 1 points: A1=(1,1), A2=(1,2), A3=(1,3), A4'=(1,4) (since (1,5) is covered). Column 2 points: B1=(2,1), B2=(2,2), B3'=(2,3). Column 3 points: C1=(3,1), C2'=(3,2). Column 4 point: D1'=(4,1). We need to assign each line Li to a distinct Ai and optionally to a Bi, Ci, D1'.\n\nLet's compute possible pairings again:\n\n- A1=(1,1) with B1=(2,1): horizontal, not allowed. With B2=(2,2): slope 1, sunny. With B3'=(2,3): slope 2, sunny. With C1=(3,1): horizontal, not allowed. With C2'=(3,2): slope (2-1)/(3-1)=1/2=0.5, sunny. With D1'=(4,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n\n- A2=(1,2) with B1=(2,1): slope -1, not allowed. With B2=(2,2): horizontal, not allowed. With B3'=(2,3): slope 1, sunny. With C1=(3,1): slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. With C2'=(3,2): horizontal, not allowed. With D1'=(4,1): slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333, sunny.\n\n- A3=(1,3) with B1=(2,1): slope -2, sunny. With B2=(2,2): slope -1, not allowed. With B3'=(2,3): horizontal, not allowed. With C1=(3,1): slope (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. With C2'=(3,2): slope (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. With D1'=(4,1): slope (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3 ≈ -0.667, sunny.\n\n- A4'=(1,4) with B1=(2,1): slope (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, sunny. With B2=(2,2): slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. With B3'=(2,3): slope (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. With C1=(3,1): slope (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. With C2'=(3,2): slope (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. With D1'=(4,1): slope (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus many possible pairings.\n\nNow we need to assign lines to cover all points. Let's try to assign:\n\n- L1: A1=(1,1) with B2=(2,2) (slope 1). Also maybe C2'=(3,2)? Let's check if (1,1)-(2,2)-(3,2) are collinear? The line through (1,1) and (2,2) is y=x. At x=3, y=3, not (3,2). So cannot. Could also include D1'? No.\n\n- L2: A2=(1,2) with B3'=(2,3) (slope 1). This line is y = x+1? Actually line through (1,2) and (2,3) slope 1, equation y = x+1. At x=3, y=4 (not needed). So L2 covers A2 and B3'.\n\n- L3: A3=(1,3) with B1=(2,1) (slope -2). Equation y = -2x +5. At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1 (not needed). So L3 covers A3 and B1.\n\n- L4: A4'=(1,4) with C1=(3,1) (slope (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5). Equation y = -1.5x + 5.5. At x=2, y = -3+5.5 = 2.5 (not integer). So L4 covers A4' and C1.\n\nNow we have covered A1-A4', B1, B2, B3', C1. Still need to cover C2'=(3,2) and D1'=(4,1). Also need to cover maybe B2? Actually B2 is covered by L1. So left: C2' and D1'.\n\nWe have no lines left. Could we adjust assignments to include C2' and D1'? Let's see if any line can be changed to include C2' or D1'.\n\nCheck L1: A1 with B2 (slope 1). Could we also include C2'? No. Could we replace B2 with C2'? A1 with C2' slope (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2 = 0.5, sunny. So L1 could be A1-C2'. Then B2 remains uncovered. Could we cover B2 with another line? Possibly L2 or L3 or L4.\n\nCheck L2: A2 with B3' (slope 1). Could we also include D1'? A2 with D1' slope (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, sunny. So L2 could be A2-D1'. Then B3' remains uncovered. Could we cover B3' with another line? Possibly L3 or L4.\n\nCheck L3: A3 with B1 (slope -2). Could we also include C2'? A3 with C2' slope (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. So L3 could be A3-C2'. Then B1 remains uncovered.\n\nCheck L4: A4' with C1 (slope -1.5). Could we also include B2? A4' with B2 slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. So L4 could be A4'-B2. Then C1 remains uncovered.\n\nThus we need to reassign to cover all points. Let's try a new assignment:\n\nGoal: Use lines that each cover one Ai and possibly one Bi, one Ci, D1', E1 (but E1 is covered by non-sunny line). Actually E1 is covered by non-sunny line x+y=6, so we don't need to cover E1. So we need to cover points: A1-A4', B1-B3', C1-C2', D1'.\n\nWe have 4 lines. Let's try to assign each line to cover two points (Ai + something else) and maybe some lines cover three points.\n\nWe can try to find a line that passes through A1, B2, C2'? Already we saw A1-B2 is slope 1, line y=x. C2' is (3,2) not on y=x. So not.\n\nLine through A1 and C2' is slope 0.5, equation y = 0.5x + 0.5? Actually line through (1,1) and (3,2): slope (2-1)/(3-1) = 0.5, equation y - 1 = 0.5(x-1) => y = 0.5x + 0.5. At x=2, y = 1.5, not integer. So line A1-C2' does not pass through any other required point.\n\nLine through A1 and D1' is slope (1-1)/(4-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed.\n\nLine through A1 and B3' is slope (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, line y = 2x -1? Actually through (1,1) and (2,3): slope 2, equation y - 1 = 2(x-1) => y = 2x -1. At x=3, y = 5, not needed. So A1-B3' line does not pass through any other required point.\n\nLine through A1 and B1 is horizontal, not allowed.\n\nThus A1 can only be paired with B2 (slope 1) or B3' (slope 2) or C2' (slope 0.5). Among these, B2 is good because B2 is needed. So L1 = A1-B2 (y=x). That covers A1 and B2.\n\nNow we need to cover B1, B3', C1, C2', D1', and A2, A3, A4'.\n\nWe have 3 lines left.\n\nConsider A2=(1,2). Pair with B3' (2,3) slope 1, line y = x+1. That covers A2 and B3'. Good.\n\nNow we have A3=(1,3), A4'=(1,4), B1=(2,1), C1=(3,1), C2'=(3,2), D1'=(4,1) left, and one line left (since we used L1 and L2, we have 2 lines left? Actually we have 4 lines total, we used L1 and L2, so we have L3 and L4 left). Wait we have 4 lines total, we used L1 (A1-B2) and L2 (A2-B3'). So we have L3 and L4 left to cover A3, A4', B1, C1, C2', D1'.\n\nWe need to cover 6 points with 2 lines, each line can cover at most one point per column. The columns are: column 1 (Ai): A3 (col1), A4' (col1). Column 2: B1 (col2). Column 3: C1 (col3), C2' (col3). Column 4: D1' (col4). So we need to assign each line to cover at most one point per column. Since we have 2 lines, each column can have at most 2 points covered (one per line). Column 1 has 2 points (A3, A4'), okay. Column 2 has 1 point (B1), okay. Column 3 has 2 points (C1, C2'), okay. Column 4 has 1 point (D1'), okay. So it's possible in principle.\n\nNow we need to find two lines L3 and L4 such that:\n\n- L3 passes through A3 (1,3) and maybe B1 (2,1) and maybe C1 (3,1) and maybe D1'? But cannot pass through both B1 and C1 because they are distinct points with same y coordinate? Actually B1=(2,1), C1=(3,1) are collinear horizontally, but horizontal line is not allowed. So L3 cannot contain both B1 and C1. It could contain A3 and B1 (slope -2). That line is y = -2x +5. At x=3, y = -6+5 = -1, not C1. At x=4, y = -8+5 = -3, not D1'. So L3 would cover A3 and B1 only.\n\n- L4 would need to cover A4' (1,4), C1 (3,1), C2' (3,2), D1' (4,1). Let's see if we can find a line through A4' and C1: slope (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. That line at x=2 gives y = -1.5*2 +? Let's compute equation: y - 4 = -1.5(x-1) => y = -1.5x + 5.5. At x=3, y = -4.5 + 5.5 = 1, which is C1 (3,1). So line through A4' and C1 also passes through C1 (by construction). At x=4, y = -6 + 5.5 = -0.5, not D1'. So L4 covers A4' and C1. It does not cover C2' or D1'.\n\nWe still need to cover C2' (3,2) and D1' (4,1). Could we adjust L3 to also cover C2'? Let's see line through A3 (1,3) and C2' (3,2) slope (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. Equation: y - 3 = -0.5(x-1) => y = -0.5x + 3.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5, not B1. So L3 would cover A3 and C2', not B1. Then B1 remains uncovered. Could we cover B1 with L4? L4 currently passes through A4' and C1. Could we adjust L4 to also pass through B1? Let's see line through A4' (1,4) and B1 (2,1) slope (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, sunny. Equation: y - 4 = -3(x-1) => y = -3x + 7. At x=3, y = -9+7 = -2, not C1. So L4 would not cover C1. So we need to decide which points to cover with each line.\n\nWe have 4 lines left after L1 and L2. Actually we have only 2 lines left (L3 and L4). We need to cover 6 points: A3, A4', B1, C1, C2', D1'. That's 6 points, 2 lines can cover at most 2 points each per column, but each line can cover at most 4 points total (one per column). So total capacity of 2 lines is at most 8 points, but we need to cover 6 points, so capacity is okay. However, each line can cover at most one point per column, so each line can cover at most 4 points (since there are 4 columns: col1, col2, col3, col4). So two lines can cover at most 8 points, which is enough for 6 points. But we need to find lines that cover the required points.\n\nWe need to partition the 6 points into two groups, each group containing at most one point from each column. Since we have two points in column 1 (A3, A4'), they must be split across the two lines (one each). Similarly, column 3 has two points (C1, C2'), they must be split across the two lines (one each). Column 2 has B1, column 4 has D1', each must be assigned to one of the two lines. So we need to assign:\n\n- L3: column1: A3, column2: maybe B1, column3: maybe C1, column4: maybe D1' (or some combination).\n- L4: column1: A4', column2: none (since B1 assigned to L3), column3: C2', column4: none (if D1' assigned to L3). Or other distribution.\n\nBut we need each line to be straight, so the points assigned to each line must be collinear. So we need to find a line that passes through A3 (1,3), B1 (2,1), C1 (3,1) maybe? But B1 and C1 are horizontal, so line through them is horizontal, not allowed. So cannot have both B1 and C1 on same sunny line. So we need to assign B1 and C1 to different lines.\n\nThus we need to assign:\n\n- L3: A3 (col1), B1 (col2), maybe C2' (col3), maybe D1' (col4). Check if there is a line through A3, B1, C2'? Let's compute slopes: slope A3-B1 = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2. Slope A3-C2' = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5. Not equal, so no line through all three. So cannot have L3 contain both B1 and C2'.\n\n- L3: A3, B1, D1'? Slope A3-B1 = -2. Slope A3-D1' = (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3 ≈ -0.667. Not equal. So cannot have A3, B1, D1' collinear.\n\n- L3: A3, C1, D1'? Slope A3-C1 = (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So cannot.\n\n- L3: A3, C2', D1'? Slope A3-C2' = -0.5, slope A3-D1' = -2/3 ≈ -0.667, not equal.\n\nThus any line containing A3 can at most contain one other point among B1, C1, C2', D1'. So L3 can cover at most 2 points (including A3). Similarly, L4 can cover at most 2 points (including A4'). So total coverage from L3 and L4 would be at most 4 points, but we need to cover 6 points. So this assignment fails.\n\nThus using non-sunny line x+y=6 may not allow covering all points with only 4 sunny lines. But maybe we can use a different non-sunny line that covers a different set of points, making the remaining points easier to cover.\n\nAlternatively, we could use non-sunny line x=1 (vertical) covering column 1 points (1,1)-(1,5). Then we have 4 sunny lines to cover remaining points: columns 2-5. Let's try that.\n\nTake non-sunny line x=1 covering A1-A5 (points (1,1)-(1,5)). Then we have 4 sunny lines to cover points in columns 2-5. Points:\n\n- Column 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4) (4 points)\n- Column 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) (3 points)\n- Column 4: (4,1),(4,2) (2 points)\n- Column 5: (5,1) (1 point)\n\nTotal points = 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. 4 lines can cover at most 4 points per column, but we need to cover column 2's 4 points, so each line must intersect column 2 at a distinct point. So each line will have a point in column 2. Then we need to assign column 3 points (3) to three of the lines, column 4 points (2) to two lines, column 5 point (1) to one line. So we need to assign each line to a distinct column 2 point, and optionally to a column 3 point, column 4 point, column 5 point. So we need to find 4 lines each passing through a distinct point in column 2 and possibly other points.\n\nLet's denote column 2 points: B1=(2,1), B2=(2,2), B3=(2,3), B4=(2,4). Column 3 points: C1=(3,1), C2=(3,2), C3=(3,3). Column 4 points: D1=(4,1), D2=(4,2). Column 5 point: E1=(5,1).\n\nWe need to assign each line Li to a distinct Bi, and optionally to a Cj, Dk, E1.\n\nWe need to find lines through Bi and Cj (or Dk/E1) that are sunny.\n\nLet's compute possible pairings:\n\n- B1=(2,1) with C1=(3,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n- B1 with C2=(3,2): slope (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, sunny. So line L through B1 and C2: y = x - 1? Actually line through (2,1) and (3,2) is y = x - 1. At x=4, y=3 (not D1 or D2). At x=5, y=4 (not E1). So L can cover B1 and C2.\n\n- B1 with C3=(3,3): slope (3-1)/(3-2) = 2, sunny. Line y = 2x - 3? At x=4, y=5 (not D). At x=5, y=7 (not E). So L can cover B1 and C3.\n\n- B1 with D1=(4,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n- B1 with D2=(4,2): slope (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 = 0.5, sunny. Line through (2,1) and (4,2): equation y - 1 = 0.5(x-2) => y = 0.5x. At x=5, y = 2.5, not E1.\n\n- B1 with E1=(5,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n\n- B2=(2,2) with C1=(3,1): slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- B2 with C2=(3,2): horizontal, not allowed.\n- B2 with C3=(3,3): slope (3-2)/(3-2) = 1, sunny. Line y = x? Actually line through (2,2) and (3,3) is y = x. At x=4, y=4 (not D). At x=5, y=5 (not E). So L can cover B2 and C3.\n\n- B2 with D1=(4,1): slope (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. Line through (2,2) and (4,1): equation y - 2 = -0.5(x-2) => y = -0.5x + 3. At x=3, y = -1.5+3 = 1.5 (not integer). At x=5, y = -2.5+3 = 0.5 (not E). So L can cover B2 and D1.\n\n- B2 with D2=(4,2): horizontal, not allowed.\n- B2 with E1=(5,1): slope (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333, sunny. Line through (2,2) and (5,1): y - 2 = -1/3 (x-2) => y = -1/3 x + 8/3. At x=3, y = -1 + 8/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667 (not integer). At x=4, y = -4/3 + 8/3 = 4/3 ≈ 1.333 (not integer). So L can cover B2 and E1.\n\n- B3=(2,3) with C1=(3,1): slope (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, sunny. Line y = -2x + 7? Actually through (2,3) and (3,1): slope -2, equation y - 3 = -2(x-2) => y = -2x + 7. At x=4, y = -8+7 = -1 (not D). At x=5, y = -10+7 = -3 (not E). So L can cover B3 and C1.\n\n- B3 with C2=(3,2): slope (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- B3 with C3=(3,3): horizontal, not allowed.\n- B3 with D1=(4,1): slope (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n- B3 with D2=(4,2): slope (2-3)/(4-2) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. Line through (2,3) and (4,2): y - 3 = -0.5(x-2) => y = -0.5x + 4. At x=3, y = -1.5+4 = 2.5 (not integer). At x=5, y = -2.5+4 = 1.5 (not E). So L can cover B3 and D2.\n\n- B3 with E1=(5,1): slope (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3 ≈ -0.667, sunny. Line through (2,3) and (5,1): y - 3 = -2/3 (x-2) => y = -2/3 x + 13/3. At x=3, y = -2 + 13/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333 (not integer). At x=4, y = -8/3 + 13/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667. So L can cover B3 and E1.\n\n- B4=(2,4) with C1=(3,1): slope (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, sunny. Line y = -3x + 10? Actually through (2,4) and (3,1): slope -3, equation y - 4 = -3(x-2) => y = -3x + 10. At x=4, y = -12+10 = -2 (not D). At x=5, y = -15+10 = -5 (not E). So L can cover B4 and C1.\n\n- B4 with C2=(3,2): slope (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, sunny. Line y = -2x + 8? Actually through (2,4) and (3,2): slope -2, equation y - 4 = -2(x-2) => y = -2x + 8. At x=4, y = -8+8 = 0 (not D). At x=5, y = -10+8 = -2 (not E). So L can cover B4 and C2.\n\n- B4 with C3=(3,3): slope (3-4)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- B4 with D1=(4,1): slope (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2 = -1.5, sunny. Line through (2,4) and (4,1): y - 4 = -1.5(x-2) => y = -1.5x + 7. At x=3, y = -4.5+7 = 2.5 (not integer). At x=5, y = -7.5+7 = -0.5. So L can cover B4 and D1.\n\n- B4 with D2=(4,2): slope (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n- B4 with E1=(5,1): slope (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus many possible pairings.\n\nNow we need to assign each Bi to a line, and also assign Cj, Dk, E1 to lines such that each line is collinear with its assigned points.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each must contain a distinct Bi. Let's denote lines L1-L4 correspond to B1, B2, B3, B4 respectively. Then we need to assign each of the other points (C1, C2, C3, D1, D2, E1) to some lines, possibly multiple per line, as long as collinearity holds.\n\nWe need to find a matching such that each line's assigned points are collinear.\n\nWe can treat this as a bipartite matching problem: each line Li (through Bi) can be extended to include any point P such that Bi and P are collinear with a sunny line (i.e., slope not 0, infinite, -1). So we need to assign each point P to some line Li such that Bi-P line is sunny. Each line can have multiple points as long as they are all collinear with Bi (i.e., they all lie on the same line). So we need to find for each Bi a line that passes through Bi and possibly multiple other points, but all those other points must be collinear with Bi.\n\nThus we need to partition the set of points {C1, C2, C3, D1, D2, E1} into up to 4 groups, each group assigned to a distinct Bi, such that each group plus its Bi are collinear.\n\nWe can try to find such a partition.\n\nLet's list for each Bi the possible points it can be paired with (including possibly multiple points if they are collinear). For each Bi, we can consider lines through Bi and each other point. Some lines may pass through more than one other point.\n\nCompute lines through Bi and each other point:\n\n- B1=(2,1):\n   - with C2=(3,2): line L_B1_C2: y = x - 1 (slope 1). Does this line also pass through any other point? Check D2=(4,2): y = 4-1 = 3, not 2. So no. Check D1=(4,1): y = 4-1 = 3, not 1. So no. Check C3=(3,3): y = 3-1 = 2, not 3. So no. Check C1=(3,1): horizontal, not allowed. Check E1=(5,1): horizontal, not allowed. So line B1-C2 only covers B1 and C2.\n\n   - with C3=(3,3): line slope 2, equation y = 2x - 3. Does it pass through any other point? At x=4, y = 5, not D1 or D2. At x=5, y = 7, not E1. So only B1-C3.\n\n   - with D2=(4,2): line slope 0.5, equation y = 0.5x. At x=3, y=1.5, not integer. So only B1-D2.\n\n   - with D1=(4,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n\n   - with E1=(5,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n\n   - with C1=(3,1): horizontal, not allowed.\n\nThus B1 can be paired with C2, C3, D2.\n\n- B2=(2,2):\n   - with C3=(3,3): line y = x (slope 1). At x=4, y=4, not D1 or D2. So only B2-C3.\n   - with D1=(4,1): line slope (1-2)/(4-2) = -0.5, equation y = -0.5x + 3. At x=3, y = -1.5+3 = 1.5, not integer. So only B2-D1.\n   - with E1=(5,1): line slope -1/3, equation y = -1/3 x + 8/3. At x=3, y = -1 + 8/3 = 5/3, not integer. So only B2-E1.\n   - with C2=(3,2): horizontal, not allowed.\n   - with C1=(3,1): slope -1, not allowed.\n   - with D2=(4,2): horizontal, not allowed.\n\nThus B2 can be paired with C3, D1, E1.\n\n- B3=(2,3):\n   - with C1=(3,1): line slope -2, equation y = -2x + 7. At x=4, y = -8+7 = -1, not D1 or D2. So only B3-C1.\n   - with D2=(4,2): line slope -0.5, equation y = -0.5x + 4. At x=3, y = -1.5+4 = 2.5, not integer. So only B3-D2.\n   - with E1=(5,1): line slope -2/3, equation y = -2/3 x + 13/3. At x=3, y = -2 + 13/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. So only B3-E1.\n   - with C2=(3,2): slope -1, not allowed.\n   - with C3=(3,3): horizontal, not allowed.\n   - with D1=(4,1): slope -1, not allowed.\n\nThus B3 can be paired with C1, D2, E1.\n\n- B4=(2,4):\n   - with C1=(3,1): line slope -3, equation y = -3x + 10. At x=4, y = -12+10 = -2, not D1 or D2. So only B4-C1.\n   - with C2=(3,2): line slope -2, equation y = -2x + 8. At x=4, y = -8+8 = 0, not D1 or D2. So only B4-C2.\n   - with D1=(4,1): line slope -1.5, equation y = -1.5x + 7. At x=3, y = -4.5+7 = 2.5, not integer. So only B4-D1.\n   - with D2=(4,2): slope -1, not allowed.\n   - with E1=(5,1): slope -1, not allowed.\n   - with C3=(3,3): slope -1, not allowed.\n\nThus B4 can be paired with C1, C2, D1.\n\nNow we need to assign each of the points C1, C2, C3, D1, D2, E1 to some Bi such that each Bi gets at most one line (i.e., we cannot assign two different lines to the same Bi because each line is defined by Bi and a point; but we could assign multiple points to the same Bi if they are collinear with Bi, i.e., if there is a line through Bi that passes through multiple of those points. However, from the above, each Bi's possible lines only include at most one of the other points (except maybe some lines could pass through two of them, but we haven't found any). Let's check if any Bi can be paired with two points simultaneously (i.e., line through Bi and two other points). For that, we need three points collinear. Let's check for each Bi if any two of the other points are collinear with Bi.\n\n- For B1, line through B1 and C2 is y = x - 1. Does it also pass through any other point? Check D2: at x=4, y=3, not D2. Check C3: at x=3, y=2, not C3. Check D1: at x=4, y=3, not D1. Check E1: at x=5, y=4, not E1. So no.\n\n- B1